I became a set decorator basically after  I went to college which I had studied  art at UCLA and at calarts and I was a  photographer so I was used to telling  visual stories i loved i was obsessed  with photography and i was working in  fashion and a friend of mine said do you  want to work on a film and I was like  yes absolutely and it was for Michael  Jermain on heaven's gate so I worked on  that film and then I worked for Hal  Ashby for four years basically and learn  it was my film school I learned all  about every department it was the 80s it  was a pretty naughty time was pretty  crazy I learned things I didn't want to  know but I learned every every  department and I was attracted of course  to the art department and one day house  said to me what do you want to do in  film and I said I wanted to be a set  decorator and he said you're fired go do  it and it pushed me to make a move and I  was very fortunate to get a film and I  committed to that art making process  with collaboration with other people and  I loved it I loved their bit of it and I  continued on fortunately to tell the  visual story requires so many different  aspects it's not just finding objects  it's doing your research and  understanding the characters we're here  to serve the story and to talk about and  show in a visual way who the characters  are so I think the misconception is that  it's a much it's it's a very large job I  mean you have so many different aspects  that you have to be involved in I would  imagine the psychology of each of each  character that's written how they live  where they live what they do you have to  do tremendous research to create that  character and it's not always in the  script of course so you get to use your  imagination which is the fun part and  you collaborate with your director and  your cinematographer and your production  designer and hopefully you go on the  right path well most immediate change is  through the internet and the speed in  which we 